format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radioactivity

lesson half-life
title: Half-life
minutes: 6
xp: 60
teaches: u2-half-life-meaning
teaches: u2-half-life-pattern
teaches: u2-half-life-group
relies_on: u2-random-single

description:
    How fast does a group of radioactive nuclei fade? You will look at 100 unstable nuclei, find the pattern, and see why half-life is about a crowd and not a timer inside each atom.

cite ref-epa-radioactive-decay
supports: Half-life is the time for half of the radioactive atoms to decay; it ranges from seconds to billions of years.

cite ref-openstax-up3-10-3
supports: Half-life and the exponential decay curve.

cite ref-openstax-chem-21-3
supports: Each radioactive nuclide has a characteristic, constant half-life.

card c03
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: About half have changed
figure: half-life-grid
figure_at: 1

body:
    Picture 100 radioactive nuclei in a box. Wait a while, and about half of them will have changed. The time that takes is called the [[half-life|half-life]]. Nothing was cut in half: about 50 nuclei changed, and about 50 did not.

card c04
kind: item
type: item
item: u2-l3-choice-meaning
connected: true

card c06
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: The numbers are a pattern
figure: half-life-bars
figure_at: 1

body:
    Keep waiting and the pattern repeats. Start with 100. After one half-life about 50 are left, after two about 25, then about 12, then about 6. Each time, half of what is left changes. It says about because each count is a pattern and not an exact number.

card c05
kind: item
type: item
item: u2-l3-fill-pattern

card c13
kind: item
type: item
item: u2-l3-choice-eighty

card c08
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: A group, not a timer
figure: group-not-timer
figure_at: 1

body:
    Remember: you cannot say when one nucleus will change. A big group is different, because it follows a pattern. Half-life describes the whole group. It is not a timer inside each atom, so one nucleus might change in a moment or much later.

card c09
kind: item
type: item
item: u2-l3-tom-timer
connected: true

card c11
kind: item
type: item
item: u2-l3-choice-left

card c12
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Some fade fast, some very slowly
figure: half-life-ladder
caption: The bars are not to scale: each step is far bigger than it looks.
figure_at: 2

body:
    Half-lives are very different. Some are a fraction of a second. Others are billions of years. So some radioactive materials fade quickly and others take a very long time. You do not need to memorise these.

card c14
kind: item
type: item
item: u2-l3-choice-buster

card c15
kind: teach
type: explain
tier: deeper
scored: 0
title: Go deeper: the decay curve
figure: decay-curve
figure_at: 1

body:
    Plot the nuclei left against time and you get a curve that halves every half-life. It gets close to zero without a sharp end. This is called exponential decay. Each material has its own decay constant, a number for how fast it decays.
